"name":"Fraudmaxxing",
"card":{"summary":"Looksmaxxing slang for using cosmetic and visual tricks such as hair fibers, concealer, shoe lifts and photo editing to present a more attractive height, face or hairline right away.","aka":"fraudmax; frauding","evidence":"unproven","risk":"low","note":"Effects last only while worn or applied; forum users themselves debate how obvious the tricks look in person.","status":null,"outcome":null,"price":null,"structure":null},
"quick":{"overview":"Fraudmaxxing (also \"frauding\") is looksmaxxing slang for temporarily improving how a feature looks with products and tricks instead of changing the feature itself. Typical examples are hair-building fibers for a thicker hairline, concealer for under-eye shadows, shoe lifts for height and photo edits for dating profiles.\n\nThe payoff is an immediate, reversible improvement in how someone presents. Users on looksmax.org compile \"guides\" organised by feature (hair, forehead, eyes, nose, lips, jaw, skin) and debate how much is too much, particularly for height.","how":"Practitioners combine cosmetic products (fibers, color sprays, tinted or concealing makeup, brow products), footwear and insoles that add height, and photo-editing apps for online images. One widely shared guide stresses choosing waterproof versions of anything applied to the face or hair so it holds up in rain or sweat. Results are immediate and last only as long as the product or footwear is in use.","risks":"The main reported downside is discovery: forum users note that exaggerated height or obviously applied products can be noticed in person, and one thread title describes feeling bad about frauding. Products carry the ordinary risks of cosmetics such as skin irritation. No published research uses the term."},
"lead":["Fraudmaxxing, or frauding, is a term from looksmaxxing forums for presenting a more attractive appearance through temporary aids: cosmetics, hair-thickening products, height-adding footwear and photo editing. The word \"fraud\" is used within the community as slang for any such presentation trick, and a user who does it is said to \"fraud\" a trait.[1][2]","The practice helps people look better directly and immediately. Hair fibers and color sprays thicken the look of thinning hair or a hairline, concealer and tinted products even out skin and shadows, and lifts or thick-soled shoes add apparent height.[1][3] Some users treat it as a bridge while they pursue longer-term changes, while others use it as their main approach.","A long looksmax.org guide organises frauding by facial region, and other threads discuss shoes for adding height, stuffing shoes, and the \"irl\" (in real life) version of the practice.[1][3][4][5] The community also debates it: one thread argues that adding several inches of fake height is counterproductive because the person is exposed when standing next to someone of that real height.[2] No published studies use the term."],
"angle":{"goal":"Overall presentation","chain":["Apply products, lifts or edits to a feature","Feature appears fuller, clearer or taller while worn","Better first impression in photos and in person"],"effect":"direct","text":"Fraudmaxxing improves how a hairline, skin, face or height reads right away, using reversible products and footwear. Practitioners use it for dating profiles and everyday presentation, often alongside longer-term routines."},
"infobox":[["Type","Looksmaxxing practice / slang"],["Also called","Frauding, fraudmax"],["Typical tools","Hair fibers, concealer, shoe lifts, photo editing"],["Duration of effect","Only while applied or worn"],["Research","No published studies use the term"]],
"sections":[
{"h":"Description","paras":["Forum guides break frauding down by feature: hair and hairline, forehead, eye area, nose, lips, jaw and jawline, skin and cheekbones.[1] The entry for hair lists building fibers, described by its author as a basic and well-known option that can improve hairline and density, especially on short hair, followed by color concealer sprays.[1]","A recurring rule in that guide is to buy waterproof versions of any product used on the face or hair, since rain or a sweaty gym session would otherwise reveal it.[1]"]},
{"h":"Height frauding","paras":["Height is the most-discussed category. Threads ask about the best shoes for adding height and whether stuffing shoes works, and others cover the in-person version of the practice.[3][4][5]","The community is divided on how far to take it. One poster argues small, invisible increases are reasonable while large ones backfire because the person may stand beside someone who really is that tall.[2]"]},
{"h":"Clinical studies","paras":["No studies found."]},
{"h":"Reported risks and downsides","paras":["Forum participants name being noticed as the main downside, along with the effort of maintaining the look.[2] Thread titles also show some users reporting negative feelings about frauding.[6] Individual products carry normal cosmetic risks such as irritation."]},
{"h":"In looksmaxxing culture","paras":["Frauding guides are popular and also seen as common; a reply in one thread says tutorials are oversaturated.[1] The term sits alongside \"softmaxxing\", the community word for non-surgical appearance improvements.[1]"]}
],
